      "title": "Passage 1: Renewable Energy Technologies",
      "section": 1,
      "content": "<p class=\"mb-6\"><strong class=\"font-bold text-lg\">A</strong> Solar energy has experienced remarkable growth over the past two decades, with global installed capacity increasing from less than 1 gigawatt in 2000 to over 1,000 gigawatts by 2022. This exponential growth has been driven primarily by dramatic cost reductions, with the price of solar panels falling by approximately 99% since 1976. Government subsidies and renewable energy mandates have also played a significant role in accelerating adoption.</p><p class=\"mb-6\"><strong class=\"font-bold text-lg\">B</strong> Wind energy has followed a similar trajectory, with both onshore and offshore wind farms becoming increasingly cost-competitive with fossil fuels. Modern wind turbines can generate up to 15 megawatts of power, enough to supply thousands of homes. Offshore wind farms, though more expensive to build, benefit from stronger and more consistent winds, making them particularly attractive for densely populated coastal nations.</p><p class=\"mb-6\"><strong class=\"font-bold text-lg\">C</strong> The main challenge facing renewable energy is intermittency: the sun does not always shine and the wind does not always blow. Battery storage technology is rapidly improving, with lithium-ion battery costs falling by 89% between 2010 and 2021. However, current battery capacity is insufficient to provide backup power during extended periods of low renewable generation.</p><p class=\"mb-6\"><strong class=\"font-bold text-lg\">D</strong> Emerging technologies promise to further transform the energy landscape. Green hydrogen, produced by splitting water using renewable electricity, could provide a clean fuel for industries that are difficult to electrify directly. Nuclear fusion, long dismissed as perpetually decades away, has recently achieved significant milestones.</p>",
